+{
+  "class_id": 3020,
+  "title": "repo-test-shells-out-to-external-binary-clean-machine-fails",
+  "description": "repo-test-shells-out-to-external-binary-clean-machine-fails",
+  "created_at": "2026-10-28 16:59:34",
+  "answers": [
+    {
+      "answer_id": 4752,
+      "language": "python",
+      "environment": "python3",
+      "version": "",
+      "solution": "# Fix Clean-Machine `FileNotFoundError: 'psql'` by Running Migration SQL In-Process\n\n**Scope:** `tests/integration/test_sidecar_first_fresh_db_migration.py` in `<project>/<project>-core`  \n**Failure:** `_run_rls_migration` shells out to `psql`; on a clean host/CI runner `psql` is not on `PATH`, so the test dies at process launch.\n\n## Root cause\n\nThe test has an unnecessary process-boundary dependency:\n\n```python\nsubprocess.run([\n    \"psql\", \"-h\", url.host, \"-p\", str(url.port), \"-U\", url.username,\n    \"-d\", dbname, \"-f\", str(REPO_ROOT / \"migrations\" / \"consensus\" / \"002_rls.sql\"),\n], env=env, capture_output=True, text=True)\n```\n\n`psql` is resolved through `PATH`. The API container already ships `/usr/bin/psql`, but the host/native QA leg does not. The image-build premise was false; the real defect is reliance on ambient host tooling.\n\nThe repo already depends on `psycopg2-binary`, which is libpq-backed and can execute the migration in-process.\n\n### Critical detail: simple protocol\n\nCall `cursor.execute(sql_text)` **with no parameters**. Parameters force the extended protocol, which cannot run multi-statement SQL. With no params, psycopg2 uses libpq's simple-query protocol, supporting:\n\n- multiple statements,\n- explicit `BEGIN; ... COMMIT;`,\n- `DO $$ ... $$;` blocks.\n\n`asyncpg` cannot provide this behavior.\n\n## Diagnosis ladder\n\n### 1. Reproduce the clean-machine shape locally\n\n```bash\ncd /path/to/<project>-core\nenv PATH=/nonexistent .venv/bin/python -m pytest \\\n  'tests/integration/test_sidecar_first_fresh_db_migration.py::TestSidecarFirstFreshDbSchemaDrift::test_sidecar_first_db_alembic_head_reconciles' \\\n  -q -rs\n```\n\nThe venv Python runs by absolute path, so only subprocess lookups break. Always pass `-rs`; otherwise a skip can look like a pass.\n\n### 2. Confirm the warm-host false green\n\n```bash\n.venv/bin/python -m pytest \\\n  'tests/integration/test_sidecar_first_fresh_db_migration.py::TestSidecarFirstFreshDbSchemaDrift::test_sidecar_first_db_alembic_head_reconciles' \\\n  -q -rs\n```\n\n### 3. Probe the image before accepting an image-build fix\n\n```bash\ndocker compose exec -T api sh -lc 'command -v psql && psql --version'\n```\n\nHere it returned `/usr/bin/psql` and PG 17.11, refuting the reported image-build defect.\n\n## Exact fix\n\n### Imports\n\n```python\n# Remove this unused import after the fix (ruff F401):\n# import subprocess\n\nimport psycopg2\nfrom sqlalchemy.engine import make_url\n```\n\nKeep only one `make_url` import if it already exists.\n\n### Replacement helper\n\n```python\ndef _run_rls_migration(database_url: str) -> None:\n    \"\"\"Apply migrations/consensus/002_rls.sql using psycopg2/libpq.\n\n    The whole file is sent as one statement, with no query parameters, so\n    libpq uses the simple-query protocol. That permits multi-statement SQL,\n    explicit BEGIN/COMMIT, and DO $$ ... $$ blocks in a single execute().\n    \"\"\"\n    url = make_url(database_url)\n    sql_path = REPO_ROOT / \"migrations\" / \"consensus\" / \"002_rls.sql\"\n    sql_text = sql_path.read_text(encoding=\"utf-8\")\n\n    conn = None\n    try:\n        conn = psycopg2.connect(\n            host=url.host or \"localhost\",\n            port=url.port or 5432,\n            user=url.username,\n            password=url.password,\n            dbname=url.database,\n            connect_timeout=10,\n        )\n        # Mirror psql: let the SQL file control its own transactions.\n        conn.autocommit = True\n        with conn.cursor() as cur:\n            cur.execute(sql_text)  # no params -> simple protocol\n    except psycopg2.Error as exc:\n        if conn is not None:\n            conn.rollback()\n        raise AssertionError(\n            f\"Failed to apply {sql_path.name} to database \"\n            f\"{url.database!r}: {exc}\"\n        ) from exc\n    finally:\n        if conn is not None:\n            conn.close()\n```\n\nDo **not** use `cur.execute(sql_text, ())` or any parameterized form; that can switch to the extended protocol and break multi-statement SQL.\n\nIf `002_rls.sql` contains psql-only meta-commands such as `\\set`, `\\gexec`, or `\\copy`, replace those with SQL/library equivalents. Ordinary SQL, transactions, and `DO` blocks work.\n\n## Verification: all four legs\n\n### (a) Clean-path run with no external binaries\n\n```bash\nenv PATH=/nonexistent .venv/bin/python -m pytest \\\n  'tests/integration/test_sidecar_first_fresh_db_migration.py::TestSidecarFirstFreshDbSchemaDrift::test_sidecar_first_db_alembic_head_reconciles' \\\n  -q -rs\n```\n\nExpected:\n\n```text\n1 passed\n```\n\nThere must be **no skip lines**.\n\n### (b) Full file with the binary present\n\n```bash\n.venv/bin/python -m pytest \\\n  tests/integration/test_sidecar_first_fresh_db_migration.py \\\n  -q -rs\n```\n\nExpected:\n\n```text\n2 passed\n```\n\n### (c) Negative control: the helper must raise\n\n```bash\ncat > /tmp/check_rls_negative.py <<'PY'\nfrom tests.integration.test_sidecar_first_fresh_db_migration import _run_rls_migration\n\nMISSING_URL = \"postgresql://postgres:<email>:5435/definitely_missing_db_xyz\"\n\ntry:\n    _run_rls_migration(MISSING_URL)\nexcept AssertionError as exc:\n    text = str(exc)\n    assert \"does not exist\" in text or \"Failed to apply\" in text, text\n    print(\"PASS: helper raised AssertionError\")\n    print(text.splitlines()[0])\nelse:\n    raise SystemExit(\"FAIL: helper swallowed the database error\")\nPY\n\n.venv/bin/python /tmp/check_rls_negative.py\n```\n\nExpected output contains a PostgreSQL error such as:\n\n```text\nPASS: helper raised AssertionError\nFailed to apply 002_rls.sql to database 'definitely_missing_db_xyz': connection to server ... FATAL: database \"definitely_missing_db_xyz\" does not exist\n```\n\nAlso verify SQL errors are not swallowed:\n\n```bash\ncat > /tmp/bad_check.sql <<'SQL'\nBEGIN;\nSELECT 1/0;\nCOMMIT;\nSQL\n```\n\nCalling the helper with this file must raise `AssertionError` containing `division by zero`.\n\n### (d) Lint and format\n\n```bash\n.venv/bin/ruff check tests/integration/test_sidecar_first_fresh_db_migration.py\n.venv/bin/ruff format --check tests/integration/test_sidecar_first_fresh_db_migration.py\n```\n\nExpected: clean, with no `F401` for `subprocess`.\n\nFull tracked scope:\n\n```bash\n.venv/bin/ruff check .\n.venv/bin/ruff format --check .\n```\n\nExpected: 260 files clean.\n\n### (e) Regression check\n\n```bash\n.venv/bin/python -m pytest -q\n```\n\nExpected baseline: 799 passed, 1 skipped, plus the two integration tests passing when PostgreSQL is available.\n\n## Summary\n\n| Before | After |\n|---|---|\n| `subprocess.run([\"psql\", ...])` | `psycopg2.connect(...)` + one `cur.execute(sql_text)` |\n| Depends on host `PATH` | Depends only on pinned `psycopg2-binary` |\n| `FileNotFoundError` on clean hosts | Clear `AssertionError` containing the PostgreSQL error |\n| `import subprocess` required | `import subprocess` removed; ruff clean |\n\n**General rule:** when a repository test shells out to an external CLI, prefer an in-tree/library path so the test carries its own dependency. If a binary is genuinely required, skip with a named reason rather than dying with `FileNotFoundError`, and probe the shipped image before accepting “install it in the image” as the fix.\n\nThe complete solution is also saved at `/workspace/solution.md`.\n",
+      "evidence": "# Evidence\n- Problem class: repo-test-shells-out-to-external-binary-clean-machine-fails\n- Model: openrouter/deepseek/deepseek-v4.1-flash\n- Solved: 2026-10-28T16:59:34.250Z\n- Verification: solution produced by pi in sandbox; see signatures.json\n",
+      "signatures": {
+        "description": "SYMPTOM: a repository integration test passes on every warm host and fails on a clean machine with FileNotFoundError for an external CLI binary. Concrete case (<project>-core QA battery, cell ci-pass): tests/integration/test_sidecar_first_fresh_db_migration.py::TestSidecarFirstFreshDbSchemaDrift::test_sidecar_first_db_alembic_head_reconciles died 'FileNotFoundError: [Errno 2] No such file or directory: psql' at the helper _run_rls_migration, which ran subprocess.run(['psql','-h',...,'-f',migrations/consensus/002_rls.sql]). DIAGNOSIS LADDER (three probes, ~2 min, ordered by cost): (1) reproduce the clean-machine shape locally instead of provisioning a clean box — run the same pytest invocation with an empty executable search path: 'env PATH=/nonexistent .venv/bin/python -m pytest <nodeid> -q -rs'. The venv python runs by absolute path, so ONLY subprocess lookups break; this is a faithful clean-host proxy and it isolates the failure to an external-binary dependency. Always pass -rs: without it a SKIP looks like a PASS. (2) confirm the same node passes with normal PATH (warm-host false green). (3) before believing the reporter's proposed fix, probe the shipped artifact: 'docker compose exec -T <svc> sh -lc \"command -v psql && psql --version\"'. Here the api image ALREADY shipped /usr/bin/psql (postgresql-client in the runtime stage, present since months earlier), so the reported defect clause 'the image build must install the client' was REFUTED; the real gap was the host/native invocation the QA ci-pass leg uses. A reported defect can be already-fixed in one environment and still live in another — classify the gap by WHERE the leg ran, not by the reporter's proposed cause. ROOT CAUSE: the test chose process-boundary indirection (shelling out to a CLI) for an operation the process can do in-band, making test outcome depend on ambient host tooling. FIX: use an already-shipped in-tree library equivalent — psycopg2-binary (already in requirements.txt, libpq-backed) executing the whole .sql file in ONE cursor.execute(sql_text) with NO query parameters, because parameters force the extended protocol which cannot run multi-statement SQL; the libpq simple protocol is what lets BEGIN/COMMIT and DO $$ ... $$ blocks run in a single call (asyncpg cannot). Keep the failure contract: wrap psycopg2.Error in AssertionError carrying the server error text so a silently-not-applied migration can never pass. Remove the now-unused 'import subprocess' (ruff F401). VERIFICATION (all four legs required): (a) clean-path run with PATH=/nonexistent -rs -> 1 passed with no skip lines; (b) full file with the binary present -> 2 passed; (c) NEGATIVE CONTROL — call the helper directly against a nonexistent database from a small script and assert it RAISES (AssertionError with the PG error); a helper that swallows errors would turn the test green while proving nothing; (d) ruff check + ruff format --check on the tracked scope. GENERAL RULE: when a repo test shells out to an external binary, prefer the in-tree library path so the test carries its own dependency; if a binary is genuinely required, make the test skip with a NAMED reason rather than dying with FileNotFoundError, and never accept 'install it in the image' as the fix until you have probed the image yourself.",
